# Project-root .env for `docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml -f
# docker/docker-compose.prod.yml ...` on the clinic Server PC ONLY.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the repo root and fill in the real value(s).
# `.env` itself is gitignored - never commit it.
#
# This is separate from `backend/.env` (the FastAPI app's own settings,
# loaded via `env_file:` inside the containers) - this root-level `.env` is
# read by the `docker compose` CLI itself, for variable substitution inside
# the compose YAML files (the `${...}` references in docker-compose.prod.yml).

# Must match the actual password already set on the live `postgres` role
# inside the `docker_postgres_data` volume - changing this value here does
# NOT change the real database password (see docker-compose.prod.yml's
# comments for why). Get the current real value from whoever manages the
# clinic server; do not invent a new one without also rotating it in
# Postgres itself first.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Optional overrides - both already default to the real clinic Server PC
# address (192.168.68.106) inside docker-compose.prod.yml, so these only
# need to be set here if deploying to a different machine/IP.
# CLINIC_FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://192.168.68.106:3000
# CLINIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.68.106:8000/api/v1

# Escape hatch ONLY - docker-compose.prod.yml already pins the Compose
# project name to "canora_clinic" (its top-level `name:` field) and pins
# postgres_data/redis_data/backend_var_data to literal volume names, so
# `deploy.cmd` needs neither this variable nor a `-p` flag in the normal
# case. Set this ONLY if a one-time `docker volume ls` check on THIS
# specific Server PC shows its real, already-running Postgres data volume
# was created under a DIFFERENT project name than "canora_clinic" (e.g. a
# machine that was running this stack before the pin existed) - see
# docs/DOCKER_UPDATE_PROCEDURE.md's "Volume identity - one-time
# verification" section before ever setting this. `deploy.cmd` passes it as
# `docker compose -p <this value> ...` (a CLI flag always wins over the
# compose file's own `name:`), so setting it wrong is exactly as dangerous
# as the ambiguity this variable exists to let you correct - verify first.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_OVERRIDE=
